   angustifolia for arsenic contaminated soil},
Booktitle = {ENVIRONMENTAL ARSENIC IN A CHANGING WORLD (AS2018)},
Series = {Arsenic in the Environment},
Year = {2018},
Pages = {547-548},
Note = {7th International Congress and Exhibition on Arsenic in the Environment
   (As) - Environmental Arsenic in a Changing World, Chinese Acad Sci, Inst
   Urban Environm, Beijing, PEOPLES R CHINA, JUL 01-06, 2018},
Organization = {KTH Royal Inst Technol; Univ So Queensland; KWR Watercycle Res Inst;
   Univ Newcastle; Int Soc Groundwater Sustainable Dev; Inst Urban
   Environm; China Univ Geosciences; Univ S Australia, CRC CARE; China
   Assoc Sci \& Technol, China Ctr Int Sci \& Technol Exchange; OPCW},
Abstract = {Arsenic (As)-based chemicals, insecticides, herbicides, rodenticides and
   irrigation with As containing water have resulted in As contamination in
   farmland, lawns and residential areas. This study focused on
   phytoremediation potential of a seasonal flowering plant Zinnia
   angustifolia for As removal from contaminated soil. Plants were
   subjected to As stress (100 mg kg(-1) soil) in field conditions for 60
   days and their tolerance was evaluated through growth and antioxidant
   assays. Root tissue was found to accumulate more As (1085.42 Rg g(-1)
   dry weight (DW)) in comparison to shoot (279.28 Rg g(-1) DW) and flowers
   (228.67 Rg g(-1) DW) at 60 d.},
