   Budapest, HUNGARY, AUG, 2013},
Organization = {Fisheries Joint Management Comm; Natl Oceanog \& Atmospher Adm; Natl Sci
   Fdn; NAVFAC; Off Naval Res; Off Naval Res Global; Acoust Soc Amer; Aquat
   Noise Trust; Discovery Sound Sea; Co Biologists; Univ Maryland, Coll
   Chem \& Life Sci; Ctr Comparat \& Evolutionary Biol Hearing},
Abstract = {Acoustic monitoring of harbor porpoises (Phocoena phocoena L., 1758)
   indicated a strongly reduced disturbance by noise emitted by pile
   driving for offshore wind turbine foundations insulated by a big bubble
   curtain (BBC). This newly developed noise mitigation system was tested
   during construction of the offshore wind farm Borkum West II (North
   Sea). Because porpoise activity strongly corresponded to the sound
   level, operation of the new system under its most suitable configuration
   reduced the porpoise disturbance area by similar to 90\%. Hence, for the
   first time, a positive effect of a noise mitigation system during
   offshore pile driving on an affected marine mammal species could be
   demonstrated.},
